## Photometry formulas: illuminance, a lamp's luminous intensity, illuminance from a point source.

formula-optics-illuminance = Illuminance
formula-optics-illuminance-e-name = illuminance
formula-optics-illuminance-phi-name = the luminous flux falling on the surface
formula-optics-illuminance-s-name = the surface’s area
formula-optics-illuminance-explanation-novice = Illuminance tells how much light falls on each square metre of a surface. The same light on a smaller area is brighter.
formula-optics-illuminance-explanation-school = Illuminance E = Φ/S is the luminous flux falling on a surface divided by its area. Its unit is the lux: 1 lx = 1 lm/m². It is measured with a light meter.

formula-optics-isotropic-intensity = The luminous intensity of a lamp shining alike in all directions
formula-optics-isotropic-intensity-i-name = luminous intensity
formula-optics-isotropic-intensity-phi-name = the lamp’s luminous flux (written on its box)
formula-optics-isotropic-intensity-beyond-curriculum = Luminous intensity is not studied in grade 7; the formula is beyond the programme.
formula-optics-isotropic-intensity-explanation-novice = A lamp sends its light out in all directions. Luminous intensity is how much of it goes one way.
formula-optics-isotropic-intensity-explanation-school = A full sphere is 4π steradians. A lamp shining alike in all directions shares its flux Φ among them equally: I = Φ/4π, in candelas (1 cd = 1 lm/sr).

formula-optics-point-illuminance = Illuminance from a point source
formula-optics-point-illuminance-e-name = illuminance
formula-optics-point-illuminance-i-name = the source’s luminous intensity
formula-optics-point-illuminance-r-name = the distance from the source to the surface
formula-optics-point-illuminance-alpha-name = the angle between the ray and the surface’s perpendicular
formula-optics-point-illuminance-beyond-curriculum = In grade 7 illuminance is only measured; the inverse-square law is beyond the programme.
formula-optics-point-illuminance-explanation-novice = Twice as far from the lamp — four times darker. And a tilted surface catches less light than one turned to the lamp.
formula-optics-point-illuminance-explanation-school = Light from a point source spreads over a sphere whose area 4πr² grows as the square of the distance, so E = I/r². If the rays fall at an angle α to the perpendicular, the same flux lies on a larger area, and the illuminance is cos α times smaller.

formula-optics-luminous-flux = A lamp’s luminous flux
formula-optics-luminous-flux-phi-name = luminous flux
formula-optics-luminous-flux-eta-name = the lamp’s luminous efficacy
formula-optics-luminous-flux-p-name = the lamp’s electric power
formula-optics-luminous-flux-beyond-curriculum = Luminous efficacy is not studied in grade 7; the formula is beyond the programme.
formula-optics-luminous-flux-explanation-novice = A more powerful lamp shines brighter. But an LED lamp gives several times more light for the same power than a filament lamp.
formula-optics-luminous-flux-explanation-school = Luminous efficacy η is how many lumens a lamp gives for each watt: about 8–14 lm/W for filament lamps (the rest of the energy goes into heat), about 100 lm/W for LED lamps. The luminous flux Φ = η · P.
